  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 252 AM CST Mon Nov 9 2026\n\nLight winds and mostly clear skies will lead to another frosty \nstart to the day, particularly in the outer suburbs and rural \nareas. Today will be similar to yesterday, albeit less breezy, \nwith high temperatures in the upper 50s to lower 60s and \noccasional thin high-clouds moving through. A weak cold front \nwill drift across the area late tonight, though a lack of \nnotable airmass differences behind the front should allow \ntemperatures to still warm into the upper 50s to mid 60s on \nTuesday.\n\nA pattern shift toward cooler temperatures and renewed \nprecipitation chances is still anticipated mid to late week. \nThis begins with a compact shortwave that is forecast to move \nacross the region on Wednesday, bringing our next chance of \nshowers (30-50% chance). There remains some uncertainty in where\nthe associated surface low tracks, which will then factor into \nthe location of greatest shower coverage. At this distance, \nlocally higher chances exist near and south of I-80. While low \nto mid-level lapse rates this far north are not especially \nsteep, any deeper convection may be able to tap into sufficient \nelevated instability to produce a few lightning strikes or small\nhail. Currently hold off on a formal mention in the gridded \nforecast, with the slightly better potential existing in central\nIllinois.\n\nThe aforementioned shortwave may phase with a series of vorts \nrounding a closed upper low over Ontario as the broader upper \ntrough descends across the eastern CONUS. One such vort may dive\nacross the area on Thursday, which could bring showers (and \nperhaps a few wet snowflakes) well inland. The resultant colder \nairmass advecting across a still warm Lake Michigan will set the\nstage for a multi-day period of lake effect showers into \nportions of the area. While there remains strong ensemble \nsupport for lake effect precipitation, uncertainty remains \nregarding the resultant low-level flow orientation (NNE vs \nNNW/NW), which will determine if and how often the band(s) of \nshowers drifts west into northeast Illinois or it if remains \nlargely over northwest Indiana. These details are difficult to \npin down this far out and will continue to monitor trends over \nthe next few days. While some models are already producing minor\nsnow accumulations, model soundings still show cloud tops \nremaining just below the DGZ, which paired with the warm ground \ntemperatures would likely limit or even prevent snow \naccumulations. Nevertheless, it continues to look like snow will\nlikely mix in with the lake effect showers at times.\n\nA strong surface high expands eastward over the weekend into \nnext week, bringing an end to lake effect showers and leading \nto another stretch of dry conditions with temperatures gradually\nmoderating back into the 50s.\n\nPetr",
